\begin{proof}
If n=1, then (n-1)!=0!=1\equiv0\pmod 1. Now suppose n>1 is not prime. If n is not a square of a prime, write n=uv with 2\le u<v\le n-1. Then both u and v occur among the factors of (n-1)!, so n=uv divides (n-1)!.

It remains to consider n=p^2 for a prime p. If p=2, then n=4 and 3!=6 is not divisible by 4. If p>2, both p and 2p occur among the factors of (p^2-1)!, and they are distinct; hence p^2 divides (p^2-1)!=(n-1)!.

Therefore (n-1)!\equiv0\pmod n for every nonprime n, except n=4.
\end{proof}

\begin{proof}
For n=1, the assertion holds since 0!=1\equiv0\pmod1. Assume henceforth that n>1 is composite. Choose a factorization n=ab with 2\le a\le b. If a<b, then a and b are distinct integers in \{2,\ldots,n-1\}, so both occur in (n-1)!, giving n=ab\mid(n-1)!.

If a=b, then n=a^2. If a is composite, write a=rs with 2\le r<s\le a-1. Then n=a^2=r(as), and r and as are distinct integers between 2 and n-1. Thus n\mid(n-1)!.

Consequently, the only remaining case is n=p^2 with p prime. For p=2, n=4 and 3!=6 is not divisible by 4. For p>2, the distinct factors p and 2p occur in (p^2-1)!, so p^2\mid(p^2-1)!.

Thus (n-1)!\equiv0\pmod n, except when n=4.
\end{proof}

\begin{proof}
If n=1, then 0!=1\equiv0\pmod1. Let n>1 be composite. If n is not the square of a prime, choose a proper factorization n=ab with 2\le a<b\le n-1. Since a and b are distinct factors of (n-1)!, we have n=ab\mid(n-1)!.

Suppose instead that n is a square. If n=a^2 with a composite, write a=rs with 2\le r<s\le a-1. Then n=r(as), where 2\le r<as\le n-1; hence the two distinct factors r and as both occur in (n-1)!, and n divides (n-1)!.

The only case left is n=p^2 for a prime p. When p=2, n=4 and 3!=6, so 4\nmid3!. When p>2, p and 2p are distinct integers less than p^2, and their product is 2p^2. Since (p^2-1)! contains both factors, it contains p^2 as a divisor. Therefore p^2\mid(p^2-1)!.

Hence (n-1)!\equiv0\pmod n for every composite n except n=4.
\end{proof}
